description:
A knife pulled through stiff paint can leave ribs across the stroke (washboard): the paint sticks to the blade, gives, and sticks again. huile shows ribs today, but by accident (murex-sdz): their spacing is the step of the stroke, and tube paint (body 0.7) shows them nearly as much as the stiffest (1.0), where the owner expects them on the drier side only.

Wanted, once murex-sdz has removed the accidental ones:
- ribs as a deliberate stick-slip rule of the knife;
- strength rising steeply with the paint's body: none in thinned paint, little in tube paint, marked in stiff or drying paint;
- spacing set by the blade and the paint (flex, pressure, body, how thick the paint lies), never by the step or the resolution: the same mark with the stroke cut into finer steps;
- irregular, as a hand makes them, not a ruled grating;
- paint conserved.

It is the same behaviour of stiff paint as the breaking of a skimming knife (the Bob Ross snow issue): worth one mechanism for both.

Alters the picture: judged by measurements (ripple along the stroke against body and step), bench scenes and every image of examples/huile/output regenerated.

A knife pulled across thick wet paint leaves fine ribs parallel to the blade, one per step of the stroke. A stroke must not show how it is cut into steps.

Repro: a 420x300 Canvas(seed=1); a dark base (Paint("#1c1a24"), load 5) knifed on in vertical pulls 120 px apart with brushes.knife("straight", 160, flex=0.5), angle=0, follow_path=False, pressure=0.7, lift=0; it lies 2.3 to 2.9 units thick. Then titanium white, load 0.3, with brushes.knife("straight", 110, flex=0.5) along [(90,50),(200,130),(310,210)], angle=-54, follow_path=False, pressure=0.25, lift=0.

Measured (wet_thickness averaged over a band along the blade, sampled along the stroke, minus its 17 px running mean):
- the ripple is in the dark layer under the white (und), period about 7 px, the stroke's step (spacing is 0.25 x width capped at 8 px, stroke.py);
- 0.126 units rms as is; 0.059 with _knife._drag_film (eng.flow_under_blade) made a no-op, and the ribs are gone from the picture;
- with the step cap halved (8 -> 4 px) the ribs are finer and the ripple 0.066: the picture depends on the step;
- by the dark paint's body: 0.061 at body 0.2 (the settling levels it), 0.126 at 0.7, 0.137 at 1.0.

Not traced further. A guess: the blade's level is set once per step from the surface under it, the pile it has just pushed up included, so it climbs over its own pile, leaves it and sinks again, once per step.

The fix alters the picture: bench scenes and the images of examples/huile/output with it.

On the default canvas over a wet dark base, titanium white with brushes.knife("straight", 110, flex=0.5), blade across the stroke (angle=-54, follow_path=False, lift=0), pressure 0.03, a 270 px pull:
- load 0.3 lays a speckled stroke;
- loads 1 and 3 lay almost nothing (a few specks on the ridges of the base);
- load 8 lays only a thin outline of the stroke.
At pressure 0.08 the same loads lay full slabs. More paint on the blade should not leave less of a mark.

Seen once, in the tests for the snow issue; the base there was thick (2.3 to 2.9 units). Not traced. Check first whether it holds over a thin base and over the bare canvas.

An idea kept for later, not a need yet: nothing measured so far points to it.

Today the paint under a knife moves by shear alone (eng.layer_speed: a Couette profile, speed rising from the canvas to the blade), along the knife's direction of travel only, with viscosity as a box blur of the speeds (_VISCOUS). The other half of thin-film (lubrication) flow is missing: the pressure the blade builds in the paint, which pushes it sideways and backwards with a flux that grows as the cube of the film's thickness (the Poiseuille term). What that pressure would do is done by separate rules instead:
- the lips at the edges of the mark (_knife._push_aside, the spill near the edges);
- the excess load squeezed out (eng.squeeze_out) and the paint pressed out at the blade's ends (_knife._press_out_ends);
- how high the blade rides, from a formula of the pressure (_knife._blade_gap, _blade_level).

The idea: solve a 2D Reynolds equation for the pressure under the blade at each sub-step (flux = shear part - h^3/(12 mu) grad p, with volume conserved), and take the sideways flow, the lips and the blade's height from it (the height from the balance of the hand's force and the pressure).

What it would buy: flow across the direction of travel (the README's limitation), one mechanism in place of three rules, and a blade height that follows from load and speed. That last point may bear on murex-sdz, if the ridge per step comes from how the blade's level is set.

What it would not buy:
- the breaking of a skimming knife (murex-dnj) and ribbing (murex-bja): those are contact, tearing and stick-slip of stiff paint, not bulk flow;
- folding and marbling: a pixel holds two wet layers, and that limits it before any velocity field does;
- ridges that stand: paint holds them by its yield stress, which a Newtonian solve levels. It needs a yield rule with it (what body and the settling approximate today).

Cost: an elliptic solve at each sub-step of each knife step (a fixed iteration order keeps it repeatable to the bit), a slower knife, and every knife picture altered.

Take it up only when a specific artefact of the present rules calls for it (lips of the wrong size or place, paint that should run sideways and does not), measured first with tools/huile/knife_probe.py or a scene like it.
